A First-Principles Generative Multiplanetary World Engine

Mathematical Foundations and Falsifiable Propositions

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek,Gros,Meta AI

---

Abstract

We present a mathematically explicit framework for simulating the emergence, persistence, or collapse of multiplanetary civilizations. The engine separates three distinct layers: physical feasibility, institutional authority, and normative desirability. The world state is a hybrid dynamical system with continuous buffer dynamics, discrete resource transformations, and event-driven failure detection. Feasibility is defined by pessimistic interval constraints and exergy-grade accounting. Authority is modeled as a state variable with uncertain thresholds, explicit decay, emergency override, and a probability of unauthorized action. Human agency is defined operationally as a weighted ratio of required human authorizations, not as a vague aggregate. The population--ecology--inequality subsystem is coupled to life-support closure and exergy through a wealth-buffered HANDY-type model with a hard extinction floor. The framework yields several falsifiable propositions, including a deterministic no-free-settlement bound and a stochastic generalization, as well as a cascading-failure condition over cyclic dependency graphs. The resulting engine is designed not to prescribe civilization in advance, but to generate conditions under which civilization can emerge, be measured, and be audited.

---

1. Introduction

Humanity has the technological foundations for space exploration, but the transition from a single-planet civilization to a sustained multiplanetary civilization is not well understood. Existing work treats planetary science, robotics, artificial intelligence, life support, energy systems, economics, habitat design, transportation, and governance as largely separate disciplines.

The framework developed here treats these components as interacting subsystems of one hybrid dynamical system. The central object is not a narrative simulation of a predetermined civilization, but a computational laboratory in which alternative civilizational trajectories can be generated under explicit physical, energetic, institutional, and epistemic constraints.

The governing principles are:

\boxed{
\text{Do not prescribe civilization; generate the conditions under which civilization can emerge.}
}

\boxed{
\text{Validate, authorize, measure, and audit---do not merely generate.}
}

To achieve this, the framework separates:

· feasibility: what is physically and energetically possible;
· authority: what is institutionally authorized;
· desirability: what is valued by specific normative evaluators.

These three layers are not collapsed into a single scalar objective.

