2. World State and Hybrid Time

2.1 State Variables

At continuous simulation time t, the complete world state is

W_t = (P_t, E_t, M_t, B_t, K_t, H_t, A_t, I_t, C_t, \Lambda_t),

where

· P_t: planetary and astronomical state;
· E_t: exergy and energy state;
· M_t: matter and resource state;
· B_t: biological and ecological buffer state;
· K_t: knowledge state;
· H_t: human state;
· A_t: artificial-agent state;
· I_t: infrastructure state;
· C_t: civilizational and institutional state;
· \Lambda_t: authority and governance state.

The human agency index H_t is not an independent primitive. It is computed from authority and action events, as defined in Section 4.5.

The world evolves according to

W_{t+dt} = \mathcal{F}(W_t, A_t, H_t, U_t, \eta_t),

where A_t are artificial-agent decisions, H_t are human decisions, U_t are external interventions, and \eta_t are stochastic events.

The transition function \mathcal{F} is explicitly non-monotonic. It includes negative feedback, resource depletion, infrastructure decay, institutional decay, and collapse.

2.2 Hybrid Event-Driven Time

Different subsystems evolve on different timescales. Orbital mechanics, life-support buffer dynamics, construction, biological growth, resource consumption, and institutional change cannot share a single fixed timestep without either missing fast buffer failures or wasting computation on slow institutional processes.

The simulator therefore uses a hybrid event-driven schedule.

Let \mathcal{Q} be an event queue. Continuous subsystems are integrated using ordinary differential equations between discrete events. Discrete actions enter as instantaneous transitions.

Critical buffers use event detection. If a buffer crosses a fatal threshold between fixed reporting intervals, the integrator locates the crossing time precisely. This prevents a buffer from becoming negative in a single coarse step.

In the language of discrete-event systems, each continuous subsystem is represented by a DEVS atomic model:

\mathcal{D}_k = (S_k, \delta_{\text{int},k}, \delta_{\text{ext},k}, \lambda_k, ta_k),

where

· S_k is the continuous state of buffer k;
· \delta_{\text{int},k} is the internal transition, integrating the ODE until the next event;
· \delta_{\text{ext},k} is the external transition, applying resource deltas from actions;
· \lambda_k is the output function, emitting failure events when B_k \le B_{k,\min};
· ta_k is the time-advance function, determined by the next event time.

This formalism guarantees that fast buffer failures are not lost between slow population or institutional updates.

