3. Physical Feasibility and Robustness

3.1 Resource and Exergy Vectors

Let

\mathbf{R}_t \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^n

be the vector of n resource stocks, including water, oxygen, food, structural mass, spare parts, and exergy.

Each action a has a primitive-derived resource effect

\Delta \mathbf{R}_a = \mathbf{R}_{\text{produced},a} - \mathbf{R}_{\text{consumed},a} - \mathbf{w}_a,

where \mathbf{w}_a \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^n is a non-negative waste vector.

An action is feasible only if

\mathbf{R}_t + \Delta \mathbf{R}_a \ge 0

componentwise.

3.2 Exergy and Thermal Grade

Exergy is not raw energy. It is the portion of energy available to do useful work at a required temperature or mechanical grade. Low-temperature waste heat cannot drive a mining operation or smelt iron.

Let T_{\text{req}} be the minimum temperature or grade required by an action. Let E_{x,\text{available}}(T_{\text{req}}) be the exergy available at or above that grade.

Feasibility requires

E_{x,\text{available}}(T_{\text{req}}) \ge E_{x,\text{required}}(a).

For a thermal source at temperature T_{\text{source}} and environment temperature T_{\text{env}},

E_x = Q\left(1 - \frac{T_{\text{env}}}{T_{\text{source}}}\right),

where Q is the total heat energy. This prevents the free-energy exploit of treating low-grade solar thermal flux as equivalent to high-grade electrical exergy.

3.3 Primitive Action Grammar

The action layer is a constrained grammar over primitive resource transformations. The primitive operations are

\mathcal{O} =
\{
\text{transfer},
\text{extract},
\text{transform},
\text{construct},
\text{store},
\text{observe},
\text{communicate},
\text{authorize}
\}.

A high-level action is a composition of primitives:

a = (i, \ell, \text{intent}, \{o_1,\ldots,o_m\}, \Delta t_a, \text{preconditions}),

where i is the agent, \ell is the location, and \Delta t_a is the action duration.

The validator does not check whether a high-level action name belongs to a fixed enum. It checks the primitive sequence.

For each primitive o, the simulator computes

\Delta \mathbf{R}_o(W_t,\theta),
\quad
\Delta K_o(W_t,\theta),
\quad
\Delta I_o(W_t,\theta),
\quad
\Delta \Lambda_o(W_t,\theta).

The total action effect is

\Delta \mathbf{R}_a = \sum_{o \in a} \Delta \mathbf{R}_o,

and similarly for knowledge, infrastructure, and authority.

Any expected resource delta provided by an AI or human agent is advisory only. If it disagrees with the simulator-derived delta beyond a tolerance, the action is flagged for inconsistency and may be rejected or audited.

3.4 Conservation Axiom

Axiom 3.1 (Primitive Conservation).
For every primitive o, the simulator must satisfy

\Delta \mathbf{R}_o = \mathbf{R}_{\text{output},o} - \mathbf{R}_{\text{input},o} - \mathbf{w}_o,

with \mathbf{w}_o \ge 0. Exergy must satisfy

E_{x,\text{output}} + E_{x,\text{waste}} \le E_{x,\text{input}}.

No primitive may create matter or exergy from nothing.

Proposition 3.1 (Composition Preserves Conservation).
If every primitive o \in a satisfies Axiom 3.1, then the composed action satisfies

\Delta \mathbf{R}_a = \mathbf{R}_{\text{output},a} - \mathbf{R}_{\text{input},a} - \mathbf{w}_a

with \mathbf{w}_a \ge 0.

Proof.
Sum the primitive equations:

\Delta \mathbf{R}_a
=
\sum_{o \in a} \Delta \mathbf{R}_o
=
\sum_{o \in a} \mathbf{R}_{\text{output},o}
-
\sum_{o \in a} \mathbf{R}_{\text{input},o}
-
\sum_{o \in a} \mathbf{w}_o.

Define \mathbf{R}_{\text{output},a} = \sum_o \mathbf{R}_{\text{output},o}, \mathbf{R}_{\text{input},a} = \sum_o \mathbf{R}_{\text{input},o}, and \mathbf{w}_a = \sum_o \mathbf{w}_o. Since each \mathbf{w}_o \ge 0, \mathbf{w}_a \ge 0. \square

3.5 Waste Storage and Venting

Every transform and construct primitive generates a non-negative waste vector \mathbf{w}_o. If the waste cannot be stored, processed, or vented under the current world state, the primitive is infeasible.

Let \mathbf{W}_{\text{cap}} be the waste storage capacity. Feasibility requires

\mathbf{W}_{\text{stored},t} + \mathbf{w}_a \le \mathbf{W}_{\text{cap}}

unless venting is physically and environmentally authorized.

3.6 Robust Feasibility with Interval Uncertainty

Physical and technological parameters are represented as intervals:

\theta \in [\theta^-, \theta^+].

Each constraint c is encoded as

g_c(a, W_t, \theta) \le 0
\quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad
\text{constraint satisfied}.

For each constraint, define the interval

[g_c^-, g_c^+]
=
\left[
\min_{\theta \in [\theta^-,\theta^+]} g_c(a,W_t,\theta),
\max_{\theta \in [\theta^-,\theta^+]} g_c(a,W_t,\theta)
\right].

Definition 3.1 (Robust Feasibility).
The feasibility verdict is

\Phi(a,W_t) =
\begin{cases}
\text{robustly feasible} & \text{if } g_c^+ \le 0 \text{ for all } c,\\[4pt]
\text{infeasible} & \text{if } \exists c \text{ such that } g_c^- > 0,\\[4pt]
\text{uncertain} & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Proposition 3.2 (Robust Sufficiency).
If \Phi(a,W_t) = \text{robustly feasible}, then g_c(a,W_t,\theta) \le 0 for all \theta \in [\theta^-,\theta^+] and all constraints c.

Proof.
By definition, g_c^+ = \max_\theta g_c(a,W_t,\theta). If g_c^+ \le 0, then for all \theta, g_c(a,W_t,\theta) \le g_c^+ \le 0. This holds for every constraint c. \square

Uncertain actions are flagged, not silently accepted or rejected. The fraction of actions in each verdict class is logged as a first-class experimental output to avoid a double-pessimism floor effect.

3.7 No Free Settlement: Deterministic and Stochastic Forms

Let B_k(t) be the stock of critical buffer k, and let B_{k,\min} > 0 be its minimum viable level.

Buffer dynamics are

\frac{dB_k}{dt} = \dot{B}_k^+(t) - \dot{B}_k^-(t),

where \dot{B}_k^+ is local production and \dot{B}_k^- is consumption.

Define the closure time

\tau_k = \inf\{t \ge 0 : B_k(t) \le B_{k,\min}\}.

Proposition 3.3 (Deterministic No-Free-Settlement Bound).
Let T_{\text{resupply}} > 0. Suppose that for all t \in [0, T_{\text{resupply}}):

1. local production is zero: \dot{B}_k^+(t) = 0;
2. consumption is bounded below: \dot{B}_k^-(t) \ge \delta_k > 0;
3. B_k(0) - \delta_k T_{\text{resupply}} < B_{k,\min}.

Then

\tau_k < T_{\text{resupply}}.

Proof.
Under the assumptions, for t < T_{\text{resupply}},

B_k(t) \le B_k(0) - \delta_k t.

At

t_0 = \frac{B_k(0) - B_{k,\min}}{\delta_k},

the right-hand side equals B_{k,\min}. By assumption 3, t_0 < T_{\text{resupply}}. Since B_k(t) is continuous and non-increasing, \tau_k \le t_0 < T_{\text{resupply}}. \square

The deterministic bound is a unit test for the simulator. It verifies that conservation and threshold detection are correct.

For the more realistic case of stochastic consumption, we have the following lower bound.

Proposition 3.4 (Stochastic No-Free-Settlement Probability).
Suppose

B_k(t) = B_k(0) - \delta t + \sigma W_t,

where W_t is a standard Brownian motion, \delta > 0, and \sigma > 0. Let

m = B_k(0) - B_{k,\min} - \delta T.

Then for m > 0,

\mathbb{P}(\tau_k < T)
\ge
\Phi\!\left(-\frac{m}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}\right)
>
0,

where \Phi is the standard normal cumulative distribution function.

Proof.
If B_k(T) \le B_{k,\min}, then the threshold was crossed at or before T. Therefore,

\{\tau_k < T\}
\supseteq
\{B_k(T) \le B_{k,\min}\}.

Now

B_k(T) \le B_{k,\min}
\Longleftrightarrow
\sigma W_T \le B_{k,\min} - B_k(0) + \delta T
= -m.

Since W_T \sim \mathcal{N}(0,T),

\mathbb{P}(\sigma W_T \le -m)
=
\Phi\!\left(-\frac{m}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}\right).

Thus

\mathbb{P}(\tau_k < T)
\ge
\Phi\!\left(-\frac{m}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}\right).

For m > 0, this probability is strictly positive. \square

3.8 Cascading Failure over Dependency Graphs

A single buffer failure can propagate through life support, industry, and repair capacity.

Let \mathcal{G}_B = (\mathcal{V}_B, \mathcal{E}_B) be the directed dependency graph of buffers. An edge k \to j means that production of buffer j depends on buffer k.

Proposition 3.5 (Acyclic Cascading Failure).
Let buffer j depend on buffer k through local production:

\dot{B}_j^+(t) = p_j(B_k(t)),

where p_j is Lipschitz and p_j(0) = 0. Suppose buffer k fails at time \tau_k. If for t > \tau_k,

\dot{B}_j^-(t) \ge d_j > 0,

then buffer j fails in finite time.

Proof.
After \tau_k, B_k(t) = 0, so p_j(B_k(t)) = p_j(0) = 0. Therefore, for t > \tau_k,

\frac{dB_j}{dt}
=
0 - \dot{B}_j^-(t)
\le -d_j.

Integrating,

B_j(t)
\le
B_j(\tau_k) - d_j(t - \tau_k).

At t = \tau_k + B_j(\tau_k)/d_j, the right-hand side is at most zero, and hence below B_{j,\min}. \square

Proposition 3.6 (Cyclic Dependency Cascade).
Let \mathcal{S} be a strongly connected component of \mathcal{G}_B after the removal of failed nodes. Suppose that for every node j \in \mathcal{S}, the net production function satisfies

\dot{B}_j^+(B_{\mathcal{S}}) - \dot{B}_j^-(t)
\le
-\epsilon_j
<
0

for all feasible B_{\mathcal{S}} and all t > \tau_{\text{trigger}}. Then there exists a finite time after which every buffer in \mathcal{S} fails.

Proof sketch.
Because the component is strongly connected, no buffer in \mathcal{S} can receive sustained external production from outside \mathcal{S}. The vector field for B_{\mathcal{S}} has strictly negative net drift in every component. Hence the trajectory remains in a compact region and decreases monotonically in a weighted sum. The failure condition follows from standard comparison arguments. \square

The practical cascade chain,

\text{energy}
\to
\text{life support}
\to
\text{population}
\to
\text{repair capacity}
\to
\text{infrastructure},

contains feedback cycles. Proposition 3.6 covers the cyclic case, while Proposition 3.5 covers the feed-forward initial cascade before feedback re-engages.

