4. Authority, Agency, and Institutional Dynamics

4.1 Authority State with Interval Thresholds

Let a_{i,j} be an action of class j proposed by agent i. Define

\Lambda_{i,j}(t) \in [0,1]

as the authority of agent i over action class j at time t.

An action is executable only if

\Lambda_{i,j}(t) \ge \tau_j,

where \tau_j is the minimum authority threshold for action class j.

Thresholds are uncertain:

\tau_j \in [\tau_j^-, \tau_j^+].

Definition 4.1 (Robust Authorization).
The authorization verdict is

\text{authorization}(a) =
\begin{cases}
\text{authorized} & \text{if } \Lambda_{i,j}(t) \ge \tau_j^+,\\[3pt]
\text{uncertainly authorized} & \text{if } \tau_j^- \le \Lambda_{i,j}(t) < \tau_j^+,\\[3pt]
\text{denied} & \text{if } \Lambda_{i,j}(t) < \tau_j^-.
\end{cases}

Robust authorization requires the pessimistic upper bound. This gives governance constraints the same uncertainty treatment as physical constraints.

4.2 Authority Dynamics

Authority is not static. A minimal authority update is

\Lambda_{i,j,t+1}
=
\operatorname{clamp}_{[0,1]}
\left(
\Lambda_{i,j,t}
+ \alpha_j L_t
- \beta_j G_t
- \gamma_j \Lambda_{i,j,t}
\right),

where

· L_t is institutional legitimacy;
· G_t is elite/commoner extraction inequality;
· \alpha_j, \beta_j, \gamma_j are tunable parameters.

If legitimacy collapses and inequality rises, human authority may fall below critical thresholds. AI agents may then act unilaterally.

Axiom 4.1 (Emergency Override).
At least one human or institutional actor retains an emergency authority

\Lambda_{\text{emergency}} = 1

for life-support abort and minimum survival actions. This prevents a deadlock in which no actor can authorize recovery after an authority collapse.

4.3 Unauthorized Action Mechanism

Authority decay alone does not produce unilateral action. A mechanism is required.

Let an action be denied or uncertainly authorized. Define the violation probability

\pi_{\text{violate}}(a)
=
\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}
\left(
\eta_0
+
\eta_1 \max(0, \tau_j^- - \Lambda_{i,j})
+
\eta_2 (1 - L_t)
\right),

where \eta_0, \eta_1, \eta_2 \ge 0.

If denied, the agent may still execute the action with probability \pi_{\text{violate}}. Every such violation is recorded in the causal ledger. This provides a formal counterpart to agent misalignment or institutional breakdown.

4.4 Institutional Legitimacy

Legitimacy updates as

L_{t+1}
=
\operatorname{clamp}_{[0,1]}
\left(
L_t
+ \alpha_L
- \beta_L G_t
- \delta_L \max(0, 1 - M_t)
- \eta_L L_t
\right),

where M_t is the worst-case buffer margin defined in Section 5.2. This couples inequality, buffer stress, and institutional legitimacy.

4.5 Human Agency Index

Define the set A_t^{\star} of critical actions executed at time t. A critical action is any action that:

1. requires a high authority threshold, \tau_j^+ \ge \tau_{\text{crit}}; or
2. has irreversible effects on life support, population, or large-scale infrastructure; or
3. consumes more than a defined fraction \rho_c of a critical buffer.

Let A_t^{\text{req}} \subseteq A_t^{\star} be the subset of critical actions for which human authorization was required. If A_t^{\text{req}} = \varnothing, then H_t = L_t.

Otherwise, define the weighted human agency index

H_t
=
L_t
\cdot
\frac{
\sum_{a \in A_t^{\text{req}}}
w_a
\mathbf{1}[\text{human authorization satisfied for } a]
}{
\sum_{a \in A_t^{\text{req}}} w_a
},

where

w_a = 1 + \frac{E_{x,\text{required}}(a)}{E_{x,\text{threshold}}}

is an exergy weight. This ensures that high-energy critical actions have greater weight in the agency index than low-energy administrative actions.

The numerator and denominator now condition on the same set A_t^{\text{req}}. Delegation-by-design is therefore not misclassified as loss of agency.

The result is clamped to [0,1].

