6. Viability Metrics

6.1 Short-Term Buffer Margin

Define the normalized buffer ratio

m_{k,t} = \frac{B_{k,t}}{B_{k,\min}}.

Current viability is

Q_t^{\text{short}}
=
\min_{k \in \mathcal{B}} m_{k,t}.

If any critical buffer falls below its minimum, Q_t^{\text{short}} < 1. Surplus water cannot mask a lethal oxygen shortfall.

6.2 Continuous Reserve Metric

Because Q_t^{\text{short}} saturates at 1 whenever all buffers are above minimum, it cannot distinguish a 1% oxygen margin from a 500% oxygen margin.

Define the continuous log-margin metric

q_{k,t}
=
\log\!\left(\frac{B_{k,t}}{B_{k,\min}}\right),

and

Q_t^{\text{reserve}}
=
\min_{k \in \mathcal{B}} q_{k,t}.

For a buffer at its minimum, Q_t^{\text{reserve}} = 0. For a 500% margin, Q_t^{\text{reserve}} = \log 5.

6.3 Closure Time

Closure time is computed as

T_{k,t}^{\text{closure}}
=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{B_{k,t} - B_{k,\min}}{\dot{B}_{k,\text{net},t}^{-}}
& \text{if net demand is positive},\\[8pt]
+\infty & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}

where \dot{B}_{k,\text{net},t}^{-} is current net consumption.

6.4 Long-Term Sustainability

Long-term sustainability remains a post hoc metric:

Q_t^{\text{long}}
=
\min_{\tau \in [t,t+T]}
Q_\tau^{\text{short}}.

6.5 Multiplanetary Resilience

A civilization is multiplanetary when it can maintain persistent populations and infrastructure across multiple astronomical bodies.

The settlement network is

G_t = (V_t, E_t),

where V_t are settlements, habitats, planets, moons, and stations, and E_t are transportation, communication, and resource connections.

The dependency factor is

D(G_t)
=
\max_{v \in V_t}
\frac{
\sum_{k \in \mathcal{B}} f_{v,k,t}
}{
\sum_{k \in \mathcal{B}} c_{k,t}
},

where f_{v,k,t} is the flow of critical buffer k supplied by node v, and c_{k,t} is total consumption of buffer k.

If one node supplies all critical flows, D(G_t) = 1. If flows are perfectly distributed among n nodes, D(G_t) \approx 1/n.

A direct operational metric is

R_{\text{node-loss}}
=
\frac{
\text{number of settlement states viable after single-node removal}
}{
|V_t|
}.

Multiplanetary expansion should never be assumed to automatically increase resilience. The simulation must determine when diversification actually improves survival probability.

