10. Conclusion

This framework formalizes the generation of multiplanetary civilization as a hybrid dynamical system with three distinct layers: feasibility, authority, and desirability.

The central operational rule is:

\boxed{
\text{Validate, authorize, measure, and audit---do not merely generate.}
}

The mathematical structure includes:

· pessimistic interval feasibility;
· exergy-grade constraints;
· primitive conservation;
· deterministic and stochastic no-free-settlement bounds;
· cascading failure over cyclic dependency graphs;
· robust authority thresholds;
· explicit unauthorized action probability;
· operational human agency;
· wealth-buffered HANDY-Space;
· a hard extinction floor;
· hybrid event-driven scheduling.

The ultimate computational question is not:

\text{Can AI build a civilization?}

It is:

\boxed{
\text{What kinds of civilizations can emerge when humans and increasingly capable artificial intelligences explore and construct worlds together under the constraints imposed by physics, mathematics, resources, time, uncertainty, authority, and value conflict?}
}

If this framework is implemented rigorously, it becomes a computational laboratory for exploring the space of possible multiplanetary civilizations.

