Appendix A. Notation and Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 General Conventions

All resource stocks are non-negative real numbers.
All authority values and thresholds lie in [0,1].
All time variables are continuous unless explicitly discrete.

Let \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} denote the non-negative real half-line, and \mathbb{R}^n_{\ge 0} the non-negative orthant in n dimensions.

For a vector \mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^n, we write

\mathbf{x} \ge 0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
x_i \ge 0\ \text{for all } i.

For two vectors \mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^n,

\mathbf{x} \le \mathbf{y}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
x_i \le y_i\ \text{for all } i.

A.2 Interval Arithmetic

Let

[\theta] = [\theta^-,\theta^+]

denote a closed real interval. The set of all such intervals is denoted \mathbb{IR}.

For a continuous function

g:\mathbb{R}^n \times \Theta \to \mathbb{R},

where \Theta = [\theta^-,\theta^+] is a compact interval, define

g^- := \min_{\theta\in\Theta} g(\mathbf{x},\theta),
\qquad
g^+ := \max_{\theta\in\Theta} g(\mathbf{x},\theta).

The interval extension of g is

[g] = [g^-, g^+].

A.3 Lipschitz Continuity

A function f:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^m is Lipschitz continuous on a set D\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n if there exists L\ge 0 such that

\|f(\mathbf{x})-f(\mathbf{y})\|
\le
L\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}\|

for all \mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in D. The smallest such L is the Lipschitz constant.

---

Appendix B. Resource and Exergy Accounting

B.1 Resource Vector

Let

\mathbf{R}_t \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^n

be the vector of n resource stocks at time t. The set of all resource types is

\mathcal{R} =
\{
r_1, r_2, \ldots, r_n
\}.

Typical resource types include water, oxygen, food, structural mass, spare parts, and exergy.

B.2 Exergy and Thermal Grade

Exergy is the maximum useful work obtainable from a system as it equilibrates with its environment.

For a heat source at temperature T_{\text{source}} transferring heat Q to an environment at temperature T_{\text{env}}, the exergy is

E_x
=
Q\left(1 - \frac{T_{\text{env}}}{T_{\text{source}}}\right).

If an action requires a minimum temperature T_{\text{req}}, then only exergy available at or above that grade may be used. The feasibility condition is

E_{x,\text{available}}(T_{\text{req}})
\ge
E_{x,\text{required}}(a).

This prevents the incorrect use of low-grade thermal energy as if it were high-grade electrical exergy.

B.3 Waste and By-Product Accounting

Every transform and construct primitive generates a non-negative waste vector

\mathbf{w}_o \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^n.

The derived resource delta for primitive o is

\Delta \mathbf{R}_o
=
\mathbf{R}_{\text{output},o}
-
\mathbf{R}_{\text{input},o}
-
\mathbf{w}_o.

If the waste cannot be stored, processed, or vented under the current world state, the primitive is infeasible.

Waste storage is bounded by a capacity vector \mathbf{W}_{\text{cap}}. Let \mathbf{W}_{\text{stored},t} be the current stored waste. The waste feasibility condition is

\mathbf{W}_{\text{stored},t} + \mathbf{w}_a
\le
\mathbf{W}_{\text{cap}}

unless the waste is vented. Venting may require environmental authorization and is recorded in the causal ledger.

---

Appendix C. Robust Feasibility

C.1 Constraint Representation

Each constraint c is a function

g_c(a, W_t, \theta) \le 0,

where a is the action, W_t is the world state, and \theta\in[\theta^-,\theta^+] is an uncertain parameter vector.

The interval extension is

[g_c^-, g_c^+]
=
\left[
\min_{\theta\in[\theta^-,\theta^+]} g_c(a,W_t,\theta),
\max_{\theta\in[\theta^-,\theta^+]} g_c(a,W_t,\theta)
\right].

C.2 Verdict Logic

Definition C.1 (Robust Feasibility Verdict).

\Phi(a,W_t) =
\begin{cases}
\text{robustly feasible} & \text{if } g_c^+ \le 0 \text{ for all } c,\\[4pt]
\text{infeasible} & \text{if } \exists c \text{ such that } g_c^- > 0,\\[4pt]
\text{uncertain} & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

C.3 Proof of Robust Sufficiency

Proposition C.1.
If \Phi(a,W_t) = \text{robustly feasible}, then for all \theta\in[\theta^-,\theta^+] and all constraints c,

g_c(a,W_t,\theta) \le 0.

Proof.
By definition of g_c^+,

g_c^+ = \max_{\theta\in[\theta^-,\theta^+]} g_c(a,W_t,\theta).

If g_c^+ \le 0, then for every \theta,

g_c(a,W_t,\theta)
\le
g_c^+
\le
0.

This holds for every c. \square

C.4 Relation to Componentwise Non-Negativity

The most common constraint is resource sufficiency:

\mathbf{R}_t + \Delta \mathbf{R}_a \ge 0

componentwise. In constraint form,

g_c(a,W_t,\theta)
=
-\left(
\mathbf{R}_{t,c} + \Delta \mathbf{R}_{a,c}(\theta)
\right)
\le 0.

The pessimistic upper bound g_c^+ corresponds to the minimum value of \mathbf{R}_{t,c} + \Delta \mathbf{R}_{a,c} over \theta. Thus robust feasibility requires

\min_{\theta}
\left(
\mathbf{R}_{t,c} + \Delta \mathbf{R}_{a,c}(\theta)
\right)
\ge 0.

---

Appendix D. No-Free-Settlement Bounds

D.1 Deterministic Bound

Proposition D.1 (Deterministic No-Free-Settlement Bound).
Let B_k(t) be the stock of critical buffer k, with minimum viable level B_{k,\min}>0. Let

\frac{dB_k}{dt}
=
\dot{B}_k^+(t) - \dot{B}_k^-(t).

Assume that for all t\in[0,T_{\text{resupply}}):

1. \dot{B}_k^+(t) = 0;
2. \dot{B}_k^-(t) \ge \delta_k > 0;
3. B_k(0) - \delta_k T_{\text{resupply}} < B_{k,\min}.

Then the closure time

\tau_k = \inf\{t \ge 0 : B_k(t) \le B_{k,\min}\}

satisfies

\tau_k < T_{\text{resupply}}.

Proof.
From assumptions 1 and 2,

\frac{dB_k}{dt}
\le
-\delta_k.

Integrating from 0 to t,

B_k(t)
\le
B_k(0) - \delta_k t.

Define

t_0 = \frac{B_k(0) - B_{k,\min}}{\delta_k}.

Then

B_k(0) - \delta_k t_0 = B_{k,\min}.

By assumption 3,

t_0 < T_{\text{resupply}}.

Since B_k(t) is continuous and non-increasing, there exists \tau_k \le t_0 with B_k(\tau_k) = B_{k,\min}. Therefore \tau_k < T_{\text{resupply}}. \square

D.2 Bounded Production Extension

If local production is not zero but is bounded above, the bound can be weakened.

Proposition D.2.
Suppose for all t\in[0,T],

0 \le \dot{B}_k^+(t) \le p_k,

and

\dot{B}_k^-(t) \ge \delta_k > p_k.

Then

\tau_k
\le
\frac{B_k(0)-B_{k,\min}}{\delta_k - p_k}.

Proof.
We have

\frac{dB_k}{dt}
=
\dot{B}_k^+ - \dot{B}_k^-
\le
p_k - \delta_k
=
-(\delta_k - p_k).

Integrating,

B_k(t)
\le
B_k(0) - (\delta_k - p_k)t.

The result follows by the same argument as Proposition D.1. \square

D.3 Stochastic Bound

Proposition D.3 (Stochastic No-Free-Settlement Probability).
Let

B_k(t) = B_k(0) - \delta t + \sigma W_t,

where W_t is a standard Brownian motion, \delta > 0, and \sigma > 0. Define

m = B_k(0) - B_{k,\min} - \delta T.

If m > 0, then

\mathbb{P}(\tau_k < T)
\ge
\Phi\!\left(-\frac{m}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}\right)
>
0,

where \Phi is the standard normal cumulative distribution function.

Proof.
If B_k(T) \le B_{k,\min}, then the threshold was crossed at or before time T. Hence

\{\tau_k < T\}
\supseteq
\{B_k(T) \le B_{k,\min}\}.

Now

B_k(T) \le B_{k,\min}
\Longleftrightarrow
B_k(0) - \delta T + \sigma W_T \le B_{k,\min}

\Longleftrightarrow
\sigma W_T \le B_{k,\min} - B_k(0) + \delta T
= -m.

Since W_T \sim \mathcal{N}(0,T), we have

\mathbb{P}(\sigma W_T \le -m)
=
\mathbb{P}\!\left(
\frac{W_T}{\sqrt{T}}
\le -\frac{m}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}
\right)
=
\Phi\!\left(-\frac{m}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}\right).

Therefore

\mathbb{P}(\tau_k < T)
\ge
\Phi\!\left(-\frac{m}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}\right).

For m>0, this probability is strictly positive. \square

---

Appendix E. Cascading Failure

E.1 Dependency Graph

Let \mathcal{G}_B = (\mathcal{V}_B, \mathcal{E}_B) be a directed graph whose vertices are critical buffers. An edge k \to j exists if production of buffer j depends on buffer k.

For a subset S\subseteq\mathcal{V}_B, the induced subgraph is strongly connected if for every k,j\in S, there is a directed path from k to j entirely within S.

E.2 Acyclic Cascading Failure

Proposition E.1 (Acyclic Cascade with Lipschitz Production).
Let buffer j depend on buffer k through

\dot{B}_j^+(t) = p_j(B_k(t)),

where p_j is Lipschitz and p_j(0)=0. Suppose buffer k fails at time \tau_k. If there exists d_j>0 such that

\dot{B}_j^-(t) \ge d_j

for all t > \tau_k, then buffer j fails in finite time.

Proof.
After time \tau_k, B_k(t) \le B_{k,\min}. By definition of failure, B_k(t) is at or below minimum. If we model failure as B_k(t)=0 after \tau_k, then

p_j(B_k(t)) = p_j(0) = 0.

More generally, by Lipschitz continuity,

p_j(B_k(t))
=
p_j(B_k(t)) - p_j(0)
\le
L_j |B_k(t)|.

If B_k(t) \le B_{k,\min}, then

p_j(B_k(t))
\le
L_j B_{k,\min}.

This is a constant but may be positive. The net drift is then

\frac{dB_j}{dt}
\le
L_j B_{k,\min} - d_j.

If d_j > L_j B_{k,\min}, then B_j declines at rate at least d_j - L_j B_{k,\min}, and the threshold is reached in finite time. If failure is modeled as B_k(t)=0, the stronger result follows directly. \square

The simulator treats failure as B_k(t)=0 for the purpose of downstream production after the failure event.

E.3 Cyclic Cascading: Componentwise Negative Drift

Proposition E.2 (Componentwise Negative Drift).
Let \mathcal{S} be a strongly connected component of \mathcal{G}_B after removal of failed nodes. Suppose that for every j\in\mathcal{S},

\dot{B}_j^+(B_{\mathcal{S}}) - \dot{B}_j^-(t)
\le
-\epsilon_j
<
0

for all feasible B_{\mathcal{S}} and all t > \tau_{\text{trigger}}. Then every buffer in \mathcal{S} fails in finite time.

Proof.
For each j\in\mathcal{S},

\frac{dB_j}{dt}
\le
-\epsilon_j.

Integrating from \tau_{\text{trigger}} to t,

B_j(t)
\le
B_j(\tau_{\text{trigger}}) - \epsilon_j(t-\tau_{\text{trigger}}).

At

t_j = \tau_{\text{trigger}} + \frac{B_j(\tau_{\text{trigger}})}{\epsilon_j},

the right-hand side is at most zero. Hence B_j reaches B_{j,\min} no later than t_j. \square

This condition is strong: it requires each buffer to have negative net drift even when production from other buffers in the strongly connected component is available.

E.4 Cyclic Cascading: Spectral Radius Condition

A more realistic condition allows production within a strongly connected component but bounds its total amplification.

Let

\mathbf{B}_{\mathcal{S}}(t)
=
(B_j(t))_{j\in\mathcal{S}}

and

\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{B}_{\mathcal{S}})
=
(p_j(\mathbf{B}_{\mathcal{S}}))_{j\in\mathcal{S}}

be the production vector.

Proposition E.3 (Sublinear Production and Spectral Radius).
Suppose there exists a non-negative matrix A\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times|\mathcal{S}|} and a constant vector \mathbf{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|}_{>0} such that

\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{B})
\le
A\mathbf{B}

componentwise for all feasible \mathbf{B}, and

\dot{B}_j^-(t) \ge d_j > 0

for all j and t>\tau_{\text{trigger}}. If the spectral radius \rho(A) < 1, then the entire component \mathcal{S} fails in finite time.

Proof.
The dynamics satisfy

\frac{d\mathbf{B}}{dt}
=
\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{B}) - \dot{\mathbf{B}}^-
\le
A\mathbf{B} - \mathbf{d}.

Consider the comparison system

\frac{d\mathbf{y}}{dt}
=
A\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{d},
\qquad
\mathbf{y}(\tau_{\text{trigger}}) = \mathbf{B}(\tau_{\text{trigger}}).

Since A\ge 0 and the right-hand side is quasi-monotone, standard comparison theory gives

\mathbf{B}(t)
\le
\mathbf{y}(t)

componentwise for all t\ge\tau_{\text{trigger}}.

The solution of the comparison system is

\mathbf{y}(t)
=
e^{A(t-\tau_{\text{trigger}})}\mathbf{y}(\tau_{\text{trigger}})
-
A^{-1}\left(I - e^{A(t-\tau_{\text{trigger}})}\right)\mathbf{d}.

Because \rho(A)<1, all eigenvalues of A have negative real part, so

e^{A(t-\tau_{\text{trigger}})}
\to 0

as t\to\infty. Hence

\lim_{t\to\infty} \mathbf{y}(t)
=
-A^{-1}\mathbf{d}.

Since A\ge 0 and \rho(A)<1, the inverse A^{-1} is non-negative. Therefore -A^{-1}\mathbf{d} \le 0 componentwise, with strictly negative entries because \mathbf{d}>0. It follows that each component of \mathbf{y}(t) crosses zero in finite time. Since \mathbf{B}(t)\le \mathbf{y}(t), each buffer in \mathcal{S} fails in finite time. \square

This result provides a rigorous condition for cascading failure in cyclic dependency graphs, provided that production is sublinear and the amplification matrix has spectral radius below unity.

