Appendix G. Wealth-Buffered HANDY-Space Model

G.1 State Variables

· C_t: commoner population;
· E_t: elite population;
· W_t: accumulated wealth/surplus;
· M_t: worst-case buffer margin.

G.2 Buffer Margin

M_t
=
\min_{k\in\mathcal{B}}
\frac{B_{k,t}}{B_{k,\min}}.

G.3 Growth Modulation

\sigma(M_t)
=
\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}
\left(
\frac{M_t - 1}{M_{\text{safe}} - 1}
\right),

where M_{\text{safe}} > 1.

G.4 Per-Capita Wealth

u_t = \frac{W_t}{C_t + E_t + \epsilon},

where \epsilon>0.

G.5 Wealth Dynamics

\frac{dW}{dt}
=
Y_t
-
s_C C_t
-
s_E E_t
-
\lambda_W W_t,

where

Y_t = \eta_I \sigma(M_t) I_t E_{\text{use},t}.

G.6 Population Dynamics

\frac{dC}{dt}
=
\beta_C g(u_t)\sigma(M_t) C
\left(1-\frac{C}{K_C(E_{\text{ls},t})}\right)
- \alpha_C C
- \mu(u_t,M_t) C
- m C E,

\frac{dE}{dt}
=
\beta_E g(u_t)\sigma(M_t) E
\left(1-\frac{E}{K_E(E_{\text{ls},t})}\right)
- \alpha_E E
- \mu(u_t,M_t) E
+ m C E.

Carrying capacities are derived from exergy:

K_C(E_{\text{ls},t}) = \kappa_C E_{\text{ls},t},
\qquad
K_E(E_{\text{ls},t}) = \kappa_E E_{\text{ls},t}.

G.7 Mortality

\mu(u_t, M_t)
=
\mu_{\max}
\min\!\left(1, \max\!\left(0, 1-M_t\right)\right)
+
\mu_u \max(0, u_{\min}-u_t).

G.8 Non-Negativity Lemma

Lemma G.1.
If C(0)\ge 0, E(0)\ge 0, W(0)\ge 0, and all parameters are non-negative, then C(t)\ge 0, E(t)\ge 0, and W(t)\ge 0 for all t for which the solution exists, provided the growth and mortality functions are bounded and Lipschitz in the state variables.

Proof sketch.
For C, when C=0, the right-hand side of the commoner equation is zero:

\left.\frac{dC}{dt}\right|_{C=0}
=
\beta_C g(u)\sigma(M) \cdot 0 \cdot \left(1-\frac{0}{K_C}\right)
- \alpha_C \cdot 0
- \mu(u,M)\cdot 0
- m\cdot 0 \cdot E
=0.

Thus C=0 is invariant. For any C(0)>0, the solution cannot cross zero because the vector field is continuous and satisfies a one-sided Lipschitz condition near C=0. The same argument applies to E. For W, if W=0,

\left.\frac{dW}{dt}\right|_{W=0}
=
Y_t - s_C C - s_E E.

This may be negative, but W is a stock variable and cannot become negative if the numerical integrator enforces non-negativity. In the continuous limit, the non-negativity of W is preserved under the assumption that production and subsistence terms are such that the solution does not cross the barrier; otherwise the model is augmented with a reset W_t = \max(0,W_t). \square

G.9 Hard Extinction Floor

Let

t_{\text{below}}
=
\inf\{
t :
M_s < 1
\ \text{for all } s \in [t-\Delta_{\text{ext}}, t]
\}.

If t_{\text{below}} exists, then at time t_{\text{below}},

C = 0,
\qquad
E = 0.

