Appendix H. Viability Metrics

H.1 Short-Term Buffer Margin

Q_t^{\text{short}}
=
\min_{k\in\mathcal{B}}
\frac{B_{k,t}}{B_{k,\min}}.

H.2 Continuous Reserve

Q_t^{\text{reserve}}
=
\min_{k\in\mathcal{B}}
\log\!\left(
\frac{B_{k,t}}{B_{k,\min}}
\right).

Proposition H.1.
If all buffer ratios are positive, then

Q_t^{\text{reserve}}
=
\log Q_t^{\text{short}}.

Proof.
Since the logarithm is strictly increasing,

\min_k \log x_k
=
\log \min_k x_k.

Let x_k = B_{k,t}/B_{k,\min}. Then

Q_t^{\text{reserve}}
=
\log \min_k x_k
=
\log Q_t^{\text{short}}.

\square

H.3 Closure Time

T_{k,t}^{\text{closure}}
=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{B_{k,t} - B_{k,\min}}{\dot{B}_{k,\text{net},t}^{-}}
& \text{if net demand is positive},\\[8pt]
+\infty & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Here \dot{B}_{k,\text{net},t}^{-} = \max(0, \dot{B}_k^- - \dot{B}_k^+).

H.4 Node-Loss Resilience

R_{\text{node-loss}}
=
\frac{
\text{number of settlement states viable after single-node removal}
}{
|V_t|}.

A settlement state is viable if Q_t^{\text{short}} \ge 1.

